WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.858 --> 00:00:11.719
is my new guitar.

2
00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:15.040
It's extra special because I made it with my dad.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.180
He owns a business that makes and repairs guitars.

4
00:00:19.901 --> 00:00:21.881
Stringed instruments have a long history.

5
00:00:22.742 --> 00:00:26.763
People in ancient Egypt and ancient Greece played plucked-string instruments.

6
00:00:28.063 --> 00:00:31.504
The first guitar was probably made in Spain in the sixteenth century.

7
00:00:32.404 --> 00:00:36.166
Early guitars had fewer strings and were shaped differently than today's guitars.

8
00:00:37.166 --> 00:00:40.507
Modern guitars have longer necks, and most have six strings.

9
00:00:41.548 --> 00:00:44.109
The top of the body is flat with a round hole in the middle.

10
00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:47.850
Making the body was hard work, and it took a long time.

11
00:00:48.991 --> 00:00:50.992
First Dad let me pick out the wood.

12
00:00:52.232 --> 00:00:54.973
This is an important step since the wood helps make the sound.

13
00:00:55.994 --> 00:00:59.275
Woods like spruce and cedar are often used to make guitars.

14
00:01:00.475 --> 00:01:03.049
These woods are strong, and they vibrate easily.

15
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.552
I chose a piece of spruce with a nice pattern in the wood.

16
00:01:07.673 --> 00:01:09.414
But this piece wasn't ready to cut yet.

17
00:01:10.435 --> 00:01:11.636
We had to dry it first.

18
00:01:12.737 --> 00:01:16.620
If the wood is not dry enough, it might shrink and crack after the guitar is made.

19
00:01:17.521 --> 00:01:20.043
When the wood was ready, it was time for the next step.

20
00:01:20.984 --> 00:01:22.325
I traced outlines on the wood.

21
00:01:23.105 --> 00:01:27.809
Then Dad used a machine to cut out pieces for the sides, front, back, and neck.

22
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.072
He did this part because the machines can be dangerous.

23
00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:34.674
Have you ever wondered how a guitar's curved sides are made?

24
00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:38.957
Dad took the pieces for the sides and quickly dipped them in very hot water.

25
00:01:39.898 --> 00:01:41.319
This made the wood easy to bend.

26
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.161
Then we had to clamp the pieces to a mold.

27
00:01:45.142 --> 00:01:46.863
This kept the wood bent as it dried.

28
00:01:47.824 --> 00:01:51.627
While the sides were drying, we cut an opening in the neck for a long, thin piece of

29
00:01:51.707 --> 00:01:55.310
metal. The metal supports the neck and makes it strong.

30
00:01:56.491 --> 00:01:57.612
The next step was gluing.

31
00:02:01.984 --> 00:02:05.726
We glued all the pieces tightly together, then left them to dry overnight.

32
00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:10.828
When the glue was dry, we sanded all the surfaces of the guitar to make them smooth.

33
00:02:11.708 --> 00:02:15.370
To protect the guitar, we brushed on several layers of protective coating.

34
00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.071
This also helped bring out the pattern in the wood.

35
00:02:20.031 --> 00:02:22.352
We had to wait overnight for each layer to dry.

36
00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:28.535
The whole process took a long time, but today I finally attached the strings.

37
00:02:29.635 --> 00:02:30.976
My instrument was finished!

38
00:02:31.872 --> 00:02:33.273
My guitar sounds great too!

39
00:02:34.274 --> 00:02:37.295
The strings vibrate when I pluck them, and this produces sound.

40
00:02:38.536 --> 00:02:40.317
The length of the string affects the sound.

41
00:02:41.378 --> 00:02:43.399
If the string is long, the sound is low.

42
00:02:44.499 --> 00:02:46.401
If it is short, the sound is high.

43
00:02:47.281 --> 00:02:50.723
When I play the guitar, I can press my finger on a string to make it shorter and

44
00:02:50.783 --> 00:02:51.584
change the sound.

45
00:02:52.784 --> 00:02:55.486
Frets help me know where to press to make the sound that I want.

46
00:02:56.266 --> 00:02:58.708
These are small strips of metal across the guitar's neck.

47
00:02:59.776 --> 00:03:01.777
The strings alone don't make much sound.

48
00:03:02.758 --> 00:03:06.460
That's where the body of the guitar, or sound box, plays an important role.

49
00:03:07.501 --> 00:03:11.864
When a string is plucked over the hole in the sound box's top, the sound becomes louder.

50
00:03:12.845 --> 00:03:16.687
The strings vibrate the wood as well, also making the sound much louder.

51
00:03:17.888 --> 00:03:19.429
Now my guitar is ready to play.

52
00:03:20.429 --> 00:03:22.291
I've even written a special song just for it.

53
00:03:23.231 --> 00:03:24.292
I can't wait to play it!
